Explain the difference between full, incremental, and synthetic full backups.

1. Full Backup

What it is:
A full backup copies all selected data every time the backup runs.

How it works:

Each backup is complete and self-contained.

Restore requires only this one backup set.

Pros:

Fast and simple restores

Easiest to manage and verify

Least risk during recovery

Cons:

Longest backup window

Uses the most storage

Highest impact on network and systems

Common use:

Initial baseline backup

Weekly or monthly backups

Systems where restore speed is critical

2. Incremental Backup

What it is:
An incremental backup copies only the data that has changed since the last backup (full or incremental).

How it works:

Day 1: Full backup

Day 2: Incremental (changes since Day 1)

Day 3: Incremental (changes since Day 2), etc.

Pros:

Smallest backup size

Fastest backups

Minimal system and network impact

Cons:

Restores take longer

Restore requires the last full backup plus every incremental since

More risk if one incremental backup is missing or corrupted

Common use:

Daily backups in enterprise environments

Bandwidth-constrained systems

3. Synthetic Full Backup

What it is:
A synthetic full backup is a new full backup created by the backup system, not by re-reading data from the production system.

How it works:

Backup software combines:

The last full backup

All subsequent incremental backups

This process happens on the backup storage, not on the source system.

Pros:

Acts like a full backup for restores

Reduces load on production systems

Shorter backup windows compared to traditional full backups

Cons:

Requires more capable backup storage

Slightly more complex to manage

Can increase load on backup infrastructure

Common use:

Modern enterprise backup platforms (e.g., Veeam, NetBackup, Commvault)

Environments with tight backup windows

Quick Comparison
Backup Type	Data Backed Up	Backup Time	Restore Time	Storage Use
Full	All data	Long	Fast	High
Incremental	Changes only	Short	Long	Low
Synthetic Full	All data*	Medium	Fast	Medium

*Created by merging previous backups, not re-copying from production.

Simple rule of thumb:

Full = easiest restore

Incremental = fastest backups

Synthetic full = best balance for enterprise environments
